**16.10. Change of Parameter**

**Negative Parameter**

If \(m\) is a positive number, let

**16.10.1**: \(\mu\)=\(\frac{m}{1+m}\)_m_i=\(\frac{1}{1+m}\)_r=\(\frac{u}{\mu_{i}!}\)_(0<_m_<1)
**16.10.2**: \(\text{sn }(u|-m)=\mu_{1}\)sd (_v_|_m_)
**16.10.3**: \(\text{cn }(u|-m)=\)cd (_v_|_m_)
**16.10.4**: \(\text{dn }(u|-m)=\)nd (_v_|_m_).
**16.11. Reciprocal Parameter (Jacobi's Real Transformation)**

**16.11.1**: \(m>\)0, \(\mu=m^{-1}\), \(\rho=um^{1/2}\)
**16.11.2**: \(\text{sn }(u|m)=\mu^{1/2}\text{Sn }(v|\mu)\)
**16.11.3**: \(\text{cn }(u|m)=\)dn (_v_|_m_)
**16.11.4**: \(\text{dn }(u|m)=\)cn (_v_|_m_)

Here if \(m>\)1 then \(m^{-1}=\mu<<1\).

Thus elliptic functions whose parameter is real can be made to depend on elliptic functions whose parameter lies between 0 and 1.

